    <string name="about_version">Version %1$s (%2$d)</string>
    <string name="about_tagline">A to-do list by date, with the day’s tasks on your lock screen.</string>
    <string name="about_section_releases">Versions and releases</string>
    <string name="about_releases_text">Version 1.0 — the first release.\n\nWhat appeared along the way:\n• Tasks by date: a day, “Tomorrow” and a month calendar.\n• Date-less tasks in a list of their own, never carried to another day.\n• Unfinished tasks of the previous day move to today on their own, marked “since 29 Aug”.\n• Completed: an archive by the day a task was ticked off, and a way to put it back on the day you pick.\n• A standing notification with the day’s tasks — in the shade and on the lock screen.\n• Search across every task at once, including completed and date-less ones.\n• Tasks with a date are exported to the phone’s calendar.\n• Voice input: hold the “+” button and dictate straight onto the phone.\n• Backup to your own server: no more than once every 12 hours and on demand, the last 10 copies.</string>
    <string name="about_section_guide">Quick guide</string>
    <string name="about_guide_text">Today, Tomorrow and Calendar are days. A task lives on its own day; an unfinished task from the previous day moves to today in the morning and is marked “since 29 Aug”.\n\nWithout a date — tasks that have no day. They are never carried anywhere and never turn red.\n\nCompleted — an archive grouped by the day a task was ticked off. Swiping right asks which day to put the task back on; the icon at the top switches between the list and the month.\n\nSearch — by title and by note, across every task at once.\n\nThe “+” button adds a task. Holding “+” and pulling up dictates a list of tasks.\n\nSettings — notifications, section colours, the bottom bar, voice input, how long the archive is kept, the phone’s calendar, and backup to your own server.</string>
    <string name="about_section_privacy">Privacy policy</string>
    <string name="about_privacy_text">Tasks are stored on the phone. The app sends them nowhere but its own server, and only when backup is on: a copy goes to the dedicated server, whose address is set at build time. There is no analytics, no ads and no third-party cloud in the app.\n\nBackup. The whole list goes to that server — behind a switch in the settings, no more than once every 12 hours and on demand. The server keeps the last 10 copies and does not read what is in them. Signing in is an email and a code from a letter, not a password; the token it returns stays on the phone and is excluded from Android’s cloud backup.\n\nCalendar. Tasks with a date are exported to the phone’s calendar — behind a switch.\n\nPermissions. Notifications are what keeps the list on the lock screen. The microphone is only for dictation; recognition runs on the phone, without the network.\n\nTask contents never reach the app’s logs or anywhere else.</string>
    <string name="about_section_terms">Rules of use</string>
    <string name="about_terms_text">The app is free and meant for your own tasks.\n\nDo not keep other people’s personal data in it without their consent.\n\nThe calendar export can be turned off at any time.\n\nThe app is being developed, so features and screens may change.</string>
    <string name="about_section_agreement">User agreement</string>
    <string name="about_agreement_text">The app is provided “as is”, without warranties.\n\nThe author is not liable for lost data.\n\nSpeech recognition can be wrong: check the parsed phrase before saving it.\n\nBy continuing to use the app you accept these terms. If they do not suit you, just delete the app: all data is stored on the phone and goes away with it — apart from the copies on the server, if uploading them was switched on.</string>
